WEBVTT

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:05.650
The irrelevance of Europe Europe is entering the age of humiliation.

00:00:05.650 --> 00:00:07.930
Europe is losing.

00:00:07.930 --> 00:00:13.070
Headlines like this have appeared in the news on almost daily basis for at least several

00:00:13.070 --> 00:00:16.770
years now, and it is not hard to see why.

00:00:16.770 --> 00:00:19.690
On one side, Europe has a war on its own borders.

00:00:19.690 --> 00:00:25.430
Now entering its thiefier and upending the continent's entire security architecture.

00:00:25.430 --> 00:00:30.910
On the other, China is pushing forward, ready to flood Europe with its products and squeeze

00:00:30.910 --> 00:00:32.410
out European industry.

00:00:32.410 --> 00:00:39.190
Then, there is the prolonged energy crisis, and finally, new technologies developed primarily

00:00:39.190 --> 00:00:44.910
in the United States, where Europe increasingly plays the role of the poorer, relative,

00:00:44.910 --> 00:00:50.350
doing the same things only more slowly on a smaller scale and often less effectively.

00:00:50.350 --> 00:00:54.990
Overlaying all of this is a problem that keeps returning like a software bug.

00:00:54.990 --> 00:01:00.150
a continent's political fragmentation and the permanent divergence of interests that comes

00:01:00.150 --> 00:01:01.150
with it.

00:01:01.150 --> 00:01:06.390
Neither the United States nor China has to deal with anything comparable and it increasingly

00:01:06.390 --> 00:01:10.030
looks like Europe's curse in the 21st century.

00:01:10.030 --> 00:01:11.830
And then come the charts.

00:01:11.830 --> 00:01:17.870
This one shows the American economy shooting upward like a rocket while Europe stagnates.

00:01:17.870 --> 00:01:22.790
Another reveals the enormous gap between American and European technology companies.

00:01:22.790 --> 00:01:26.630
The picture seems obvious. Europe is losing.

00:01:26.630 --> 00:01:28.070
But is it really?

00:01:28.070 --> 00:01:33.070
What if part of this picture reflects not so much the actual condition of Europe, but

00:01:33.070 --> 00:01:38.030
what we choose to measure and what criteria we use to define success?

00:01:38.030 --> 00:01:43.070
Once we step slightly outside the conventional framework, it quickly becomes clear that Europe

00:01:43.070 --> 00:02:08.050
may actually be doing considerably better than many people think.

00:02:08.050 --> 00:02:10.610
Then we think about global financial markets.

00:02:10.610 --> 00:02:13.530
Walls treat is often the first thing that comes to mind.

00:02:13.530 --> 00:02:17.210
The S&P 500, Nasdaq, the world's largest tech companies.

00:02:17.210 --> 00:02:20.530
The US market attracts a huge share of investors' attention today.

00:02:20.530 --> 00:02:23.490
But the stock market isn't just about the United States.

00:02:23.490 --> 00:02:28.130
Europe has its own global leaders and one of the indices offering broad exposure to the

00:02:28.130 --> 00:02:34.330
European market is the stock's Europe 600, which tracks 600 large, mid-size and smaller

00:02:34.330 --> 00:02:36.530
companies across European economies.

00:02:36.530 --> 00:02:40.730
And that is exactly why I used the XTB app, a partner of this channel.

00:02:40.730 --> 00:02:45.970
Here I can invest broadly in the entire stock's Europe 600 index, or in specific sectors

00:02:45.970 --> 00:02:47.490
of the European economy.

00:02:47.490 --> 00:02:53.610
More than 11,800 instruments are at your disposal, alongside daily expert analysis and the latest

00:02:53.610 --> 00:02:56.650
market news which help you make smarter decisions.

