involve risk. Invest, responsibly.

00:03:15.640 --> 00:03:20.480
Let's establish one thing at the outset. We ourselves have repeatedly discussed the

00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:27.680
many problems Europe has faced in recent years. They were in the East, China, Energy, Technology.

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:33.760
This episode is not going to argue that these challenges are trivial, quite the opposite.

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:39.040
But today's discourse has a tendency to latch onto extremes and apply them to much broader

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:42.680
realities, which can seriously distort the picture.

00:03:42.680 --> 00:03:48.200
So our story about Europe today will quite deliberately be a little different.

00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:50.520
Let's begin with the numbers.

00:03:50.520 --> 00:03:55.160
If we constantly hear that Europe is losing and that its slide into irrelevance is almost

00:03:55.160 --> 00:04:00.400
inevitable, then macroeconomic indicators and broader economic data should confirm that

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:02.600
story.

00:04:02.600 --> 00:04:08.060
And yet, as economists, Daniel Kral points out, despite its problems in the energy market,

00:04:08.060 --> 00:04:12.780
Europe is clearly experiencing what he calls a quiet boom.

00:04:12.780 --> 00:04:18.180
As many as nine EU countries grew faster than the United States in the second quarter, Slovenia

00:04:18.180 --> 00:04:23.420
then Marklituania, Poland, Sweden and Spain were among the leaders.

00:04:23.420 --> 00:04:26.820
As Kral puts it, the European economy is not in crisis.

00:04:26.820 --> 00:04:30.060
France and Germany are dragging the rest down.

00:04:30.060 --> 00:04:35.320
And even in the EU's largest economy, a country that concentrates many of Europe's problems

00:04:35.320 --> 00:04:39.920
in one place, sentiment is somewhat beginning to improve.

00:04:39.920 --> 00:04:45.520
The plant 40 billion euro fiscal stimulus in Germany, something many analysts have been

00:04:45.520 --> 00:04:52.520
demanding, led the IFO Institute to raise its forecast for the German growth in 2026 from

00:04:52.520 --> 00:04:56.000
0.8% to 1.4%.

00:04:56.000 --> 00:05:02.720
In July, German industrial orders rose 13% ironier, suggesting that warmer economic winds

00:05:02.720 --> 00:05:05.520
may be finally arriving.

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:10.760
But an even larger injection of German capital could come from the private sector.

00:05:10.760 --> 00:05:17.920
Germany is currently reforming its pension system, drawing inspiration from the American 401K.

00:05:17.920 --> 00:05:24.040
The goal is finally to open the door for German savings to flow much more broadly into capital

00:05:24.040 --> 00:05:30.600
markets, until now the structure of the system not only pushed Savers toward insurance companies

00:05:30.600 --> 00:05:35.040
and so-called guaranty-dreaster pensions.

00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:39.480
And this matters because we are talking about hundreds of billions of euros.

00:05:39.480 --> 00:05:45.560
Morgan Stanley estimates that the reforms could generate 40 billion euros in annual inflows

00:05:45.560 --> 00:05:47.520
into capital markets.

00:05:47.520 --> 00:05:54.560
The Vanguard believes the new accounts could attract 150 billion euros over 5 years,

00:05:54.560 --> 00:06:00.420
that is roughly as much as the research system accumulated over more than 2 decades.

00:06:00.420 --> 00:06:05.760
The obvious question is where this money will go, but if Germans, known for a certain degree

00:06:05.760 --> 00:06:11.000
of economic patriotism, decide to direct more of their savings into domestic or European

00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:12.320
equities.

00:06:12.320 --> 00:06:18.080
It could provide German national champions with exactly the kind of turbocharged they desperately

00:06:18.080 --> 00:06:19.800
need.

00:06:19.800 --> 00:06:23.920
But capital is also flowing into Europe from abroad.

00:06:23.920 --> 00:06:29.600
As valuations of American technology companies have searched, so has investor unease about

00:06:29.600 --> 00:06:32.080
how far they can keep climbing.

00:06:32.080 --> 00:06:37.040
That uncertainty has helped push a record wave of capital toward Europe, which is increasingly

00:06:37.040 --> 00:06:43.060
seen as the more traditional, cheaper and therefore relatively safer market.

00:06:43.060 --> 00:06:47.480
Global portfolios have become extraordinarily dependent on Wall Street, particularly

00:06:47.480 --> 00:06:51.000
on a handful of giant technology and AI companies.

00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:57.800
According to BlackRock, European funds attracted 92 billion dollars last year, roughly as much

00:06:57.800 --> 00:07:01.360
as they had drawn in over the entire previous decade.

00:07:01.360 --> 00:07:07.580
The firm also knows that the S&P 500 trades at an average price to earnings ratio around

00:07:07.580 --> 00:07:11.960
30% higher than the European stocks 600.

00:07:11.960 --> 00:07:17.040
But simply, American equities are considerably more expensive.

00:07:17.040 --> 00:07:24.200
After a record 2025, interest in Europe remains strong during the first half of 2026.

00:07:24.200 --> 00:07:30.960
Goldman Sachs estimates that inflows into European equities were the highest since 2021 with

00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:35.360
foreign investors responsible for much of the increase.

00:07:35.360 --> 00:07:42.080
In the week ending August 26, global equity funds recorded net outflows of nearly $6

00:07:42.080 --> 00:07:47.360
billion, including more than 22 billion living-day US market.

00:07:47.360 --> 00:07:51.560
Europe by contrast attracted nearly $8 billion.

00:07:51.560 --> 00:08:00.080
A week earlier, European funds received another $4.7 billion, meaning $12.6 billion in fresh net

00:08:00.080 --> 00:08:05.600
capital flowed into Europe during a period of clearly deteriorating global sentiment.

00:08:06.640 --> 00:08:12.160
The same trend is visible in the stocks Europe 600, which has continued to climb and

00:08:12.160 --> 00:08:19.280
regularly set new highs. For 2026 alone, the S&P 500 remains slightly ahead,

00:08:19.280 --> 00:08:25.520
up around 12% compared with roughly 10% for the stocks. But over the year and a half,

00:08:25.520 --> 00:08:31.440
Since the beginning of 2025, the European index has killed outperformed its American counterpart.

00:08:32.080 --> 00:08:38.320
The stocks, 600, rose around 48%, compared with 30% for the S&P.

00:08:39.360 --> 00:08:43.280
These positive trends are also showing up in corporate results.

00:08:43.280 --> 00:08:48.960
The second quarter brought a clear rebound in revenues and profits among companies in the stocks

00:08:48.960 --> 00:08:56.640
600 Europe. Revenue rose by more than 9% year on year while profits jumped by 19%.

00:08:56.640 --> 00:09:02.160
The surge in the energy sector was not really driven in part by higher commodity prices.

00:09:03.280 --> 00:09:09.040
Alondon's stock exchange report presents similar numbers. Analyst Tudging their D-Lone

00:09:09.040 --> 00:09:16.320
notes that even excluding energy, revenue growth was close to 7%. While profits increased by 13%.

00:09:16.320 --> 00:09:22.340
6-8% of companies reported better than expected revenues.

00:09:22.340 --> 00:09:27.540
On the other hand, the recovery has not yet translated into comparable increase in investment,

00:09:27.540 --> 00:09:32.740
although the investment typically lags behind revenues and profits.

00:09:32.740 --> 00:09:35.980
Economic activity in the Eurozone also looks stable.

00:09:35.980 --> 00:09:43.640
In August, the composite PMI combining manufacturing and services stood at 52, closed its long-term

00:09:43.640 --> 00:09:49.960
average of 52.3. According to Reuters, this suggests that the private sector is expanding

00:09:49.960 --> 00:09:57.120
at a steady, if unspectacular, pace, as a reminder a PMI reading above 50 indicates growth

00:09:57.120 --> 00:09:58.960
in activity.

00:09:58.960 --> 00:10:04.160
That said, the problems in the automotive sector are real, and manufacturers, particularly

00:10:04.160 --> 00:10:08.280
German ones, need to adapt to the challenge posed by China.

00:10:08.280 --> 00:10:16.280
Volkswagen recently announced another 50,000 job cuts, taking its planned total to around 100,000 positions.

00:10:17.240 --> 00:10:23.480
Paradoxically, however, despite hundreds of headlines about mass layoffs at European companies,

00:10:23.480 --> 00:10:29.800
the European Union currently has the highest employment rate since records began in 2009.

00:10:29.800 --> 00:10:39.520
According to Eurostad, almost 198 million people were employed in the EU in 2025. Among

00:10:39.520 --> 00:10:47.340
people aged 20-64, the employment rate exceeded 76%. And that is clearly higher than in

00:10:47.340 --> 00:10:55.500
the United States at 71.6% and above the OECD average of around 70%.

00:10:55.500 --> 00:11:01.260
of this increase, both in employment and corporate performance, comes from the defense industry,

00:11:01.260 --> 00:11:06.300
which for obvious reasons is experiencing a genuine boom.

00:11:06.300 --> 00:11:12.380
According to the European Defense Agency, defense spending by the EU's 27 member states

00:11:12.380 --> 00:11:20.580
reached 418 billion euros in 2025, an increase of 20% year-on-year.

00:11:20.580 --> 00:11:28.440
In this year, spending is expected to rise to 454 billion equivalent to around 2.4% of the

00:11:28.440 --> 00:11:30.560
blogs GDP.

00:11:30.560 --> 00:11:36.300
Looking strictly at officially reported figures that would place EU defense spending above

00:11:36.300 --> 00:11:44.700
the combined military expenditure of Russian China, estimated at roughly 380 billion euros.

00:11:44.700 --> 00:11:50.300
Of course, neither country is famous for perfect transparency when it comes to military budgets.

00:11:50.300 --> 00:11:53.020
of comparison requires some caution.

00:11:53.020 --> 00:11:58.420
The United States remains in a different league, spending close to a trillion dollars a year

00:11:58.420 --> 00:12:00.340
on defense.

00:12:00.340 --> 00:12:06.220
On the other hand, if we broaden European category to include countries outside the EU, which

00:12:06.220 --> 00:12:12.100
often act alongside it on collective defense, such as the United Kingdom and Norway, then

00:12:12.100 --> 00:12:19.140
the claim that Europe spends more than Moscow and Beijing combined becomes entirely plausible.

00:12:19.140 --> 00:12:24.380
Actually, spending alone does not determine the strength or effectiveness of an army, but

00:12:24.380 --> 00:12:26.660
let's not fool ourselves either.

00:12:26.660 --> 00:12:32.360
Money is an essential part of the process, particularly when warfare itself is undergoing

00:12:32.360 --> 00:12:35.740
a technological revolution.

00:12:35.740 --> 00:12:41.620
All of these numbers fit rather poorly, with an entity that some commentators already describe

00:12:41.620 --> 00:12:46.380
as defeated, irrelevant, and approaching collapse.

00:12:46.380 --> 00:12:51.540
Those arguments are often accompanied by charts like this one, showing the divergence between

00:12:51.540 --> 00:12:54.940
EU and US GDP over the past years.

00:12:54.940 --> 00:12:59.540
Not so long ago, Europe exceed the United States in nominal GDP.

00:12:59.540 --> 00:13:03.740
Today it trails by almost 30%.

00:13:03.740 --> 00:13:09.380
Even more striking are comparisons between American states and Europe's richest economies.

00:13:09.380 --> 00:13:14.740
On these charts, even the wealthiest European societies barely reached the lower end of the

00:13:14.740 --> 00:13:21.860
American distribution, while most EU countries appear significantly poorer than every US state.

00:13:22.660 --> 00:13:27.540
The problem is that the subjective impressions of people who have actually experienced

00:13:27.540 --> 00:13:32.580
standards of living in both places often do not seem to confirm this picture.

00:13:33.540 --> 00:13:38.260
So is there something more going on here, or is that merely anecdotal evidence?

00:13:38.260 --> 00:13:45.080
Americans, American Nobel laureate Paul Krugman, clearly leans toward the first explanation.

00:13:45.080 --> 00:13:50.440
Into articles published in May, with the telling titles, he is European economic decline

00:13:50.440 --> 00:13:55.760
and later more forcefully challenging the narrative of European decline, Krugman opened

00:13:55.760 --> 00:14:00.840
something of a Pandora's box and triggered a substantial debate.

00:14:00.840 --> 00:14:06.240
And that debate brings us directly to the heart of the argument over Europe's condition.

00:14:06.240 --> 00:14:12.200
In the first article, Krukman begins with the dominant narrative, Europe is stagnating,

00:14:12.200 --> 00:14:18.600
falling visibly behind the United States and becoming a kind of museum of the world.

00:14:18.600 --> 00:14:24.000
But he immediately adds several complications, life expectancy in Europe is significantly

00:14:24.000 --> 00:14:25.000
higher.

00:14:25.000 --> 00:14:30.400
The use of modern technologies by individuals and companies is broadly similar on both sides

00:14:30.400 --> 00:14:35.880
of the Atlantic, even though those technologies are more often developed in the United States.

00:14:35.880 --> 00:14:39.720
inequality is considerably higher in America.

00:14:39.720 --> 00:14:44.920
And some services that Americans purchase privately are provided by the state in Europe, meaning

00:14:44.920 --> 00:14:51.320
simple GDP or income comparisons do not capture the full difference in consumption.

00:14:51.320 --> 00:14:56.320
His first major argument, however, concerns productivity.

00:14:56.320 --> 00:15:03.320
German GDP per capita is around 14% below the American level, while French GDP per capita

00:15:03.320 --> 00:15:06.360
trails by as much as 27%.

00:15:06.360 --> 00:15:13.440
Yet, German hourly productivity is almost identical to that of the United States and French productivity

00:15:13.440 --> 00:15:17.280
is only around 9% lower.

00:15:17.280 --> 00:15:19.400
So where does the gap come from?

00:15:19.400 --> 00:15:24.120
According to Krugman, much of it reflects a social choice.

00:15:24.120 --> 00:15:30.240
Western Europeans do not produce dramatically less value per hour than Americans.

00:15:30.240 --> 00:15:32.720
Productivity data make that clear.

00:15:32.720 --> 00:15:38.080
consume more of their productivity gains in the form of income and goods. Europeans consume

00:15:38.080 --> 00:15:41.600
more of them in the form of leisure and vacation.

00:15:41.600 --> 00:15:47.440
Krugemann gives the example of Alabama. Alabama is poorer than the US average, largely because

00:15:47.440 --> 00:15:54.160
its productivity is much lower than the American average. France, by contrast, has lower GDP per capita

00:15:54.160 --> 00:16:01.120
largely because French people work fewer hours. But that is only one half of his central argument.

00:16:01.120 --> 00:16:08.640
The second concerns our fixation on measuring national output at constant prices.

00:16:08.640 --> 00:16:13.920
The standard chart that usually accompanies the narrative of European decline.

00:16:13.920 --> 00:16:19.920
On such charts, American GDP shoots into the stratosphere, while European economies stagnate

00:16:19.920 --> 00:16:22.400
or slide downward.

00:16:22.400 --> 00:16:28.000
But if we compare the actual value of American and European production using current purchasing

00:16:28.000 --> 00:16:33.200
power parity, Europe's relative position has barely changed.

00:16:33.200 --> 00:16:40.640
In 2001, French GDP per capita measured this way was equivalent to 74% of the American

00:16:40.640 --> 00:16:41.640
level.

00:16:41.640 --> 00:16:45.480
In 2024, it was 73%.

00:16:45.480 --> 00:16:48.240
Germany actually closed part of the gap.

00:16:48.240 --> 00:16:54.720
Moving from 78% in 2001 to 85% in 2024.

00:16:54.720 --> 00:16:59.760
taken literally, that could even suggest that Germany, not the United States, played the

00:16:59.760 --> 00:17:03.200
past quarter century more effectively.

00:17:03.200 --> 00:17:06.320
But this creates an apparent paradox.

00:17:06.320 --> 00:17:12.840
How could the United States have grown much faster for 25 years if Europe's relative purchasing

00:17:12.840 --> 00:17:16.120
power barely deteriorated?

00:17:16.120 --> 00:17:21.120
According to Krugman, the answer lies in America's technology sector.

00:17:21.120 --> 00:17:26.660
He knows that the US productivity growth advantage is extraordinarily concentrated in information

00:17:26.660 --> 00:17:27.660
technology.

00:17:27.660 --> 00:17:32.400
Americans produce at this proportionately large quantity of technological goods.

00:17:32.400 --> 00:17:38.560
The real output raises extremely quickly, while the relative price of that output falls just

00:17:38.560 --> 00:17:40.200
as dramatically.

00:17:40.200 --> 00:17:45.200
The country producing the technology captures the statistical increase in productivity, but

00:17:45.200 --> 00:17:50.720
it does not necessarily capture the entire economic benefit of that technology.

00:17:50.720 --> 00:17:55.240
competitions, spreads access and pushes prices downward.

00:17:55.240 --> 00:18:00.160
In other words, you do not need to be the country where technology is invented to capture

00:18:00.160 --> 00:18:03.440
a large share of its economic benefits.

00:18:03.440 --> 00:18:09.840
The response came quickly, above all from Spanish economist Luis Garicano and widely read

00:18:09.840 --> 00:18:11.720
No Asmeth.

00:18:11.720 --> 00:18:15.200
We will not get deep into the technical details here.

00:18:15.200 --> 00:18:18.560
Anyone interested can follow the exchange themselves.

00:18:18.560 --> 00:18:23.280
the Garicano's main objections can be reduced to several points.

00:18:23.280 --> 00:18:29.480
He argued that GDPPP is a worse measure for comparing countries over time than constant

00:18:29.480 --> 00:18:31.040
price GDP.

00:18:31.040 --> 00:18:35.880
He argued that the high profits generated by the technology sector spill into the rest

00:18:35.880 --> 00:18:40.200
of the non-tech economy in ways Europe cannot fully capture.

00:18:40.200 --> 00:18:44.560
He pointed out that the market capitalization of technology companies and therefore their

00:18:44.560 --> 00:18:47.720
returns are growing to owners of capital.

00:18:47.720 --> 00:18:50.280
remain largely in American hands.

00:18:50.280 --> 00:18:55.220
And finally he argued that one technological revolution creates the conditions for the

00:18:55.220 --> 00:18:59.080
next revolution to occur in the same place.

00:18:59.080 --> 00:19:04.880
Smith added that simply not losing ground should not itself be described as success, and

00:19:04.880 --> 00:19:07.880
that Americans consume more.

00:19:07.880 --> 00:19:13.760
Kroegman returned to the debate on May 21st in his second article where he openly conceded

00:19:13.760 --> 00:19:15.200
the obvious point.

00:19:15.200 --> 00:19:19.600
Yes, Americans are materially richer.

00:19:19.600 --> 00:19:25.760
They have more things, larger and more expensive cars, larger and more expensive houses.

00:19:25.760 --> 00:19:31.100
Europeans on average have more free time, more vacation, greater social security and longer

00:19:31.100 --> 00:19:32.600
lives.

00:19:32.600 --> 00:19:33.920
Cool leaves better?

00:19:33.920 --> 00:19:38.640
Krogman asks the question rhetorically and concludes that in his view, it is not purely

00:19:38.640 --> 00:19:40.520
an economic question.

00:19:40.520 --> 00:19:42.480
It is a question of preference.

00:19:42.480 --> 00:19:48.280
Still, the economies would hardly be satisfied without returning to the measurement issue.

00:19:48.280 --> 00:19:53.160
This time instead of Germany and France, he looks at the entire Eurozone.

00:19:53.160 --> 00:19:54.960
He presents two charts.

00:19:54.960 --> 00:20:01.100
GDP per capita at constant prices clearly shows Europe falling behind and its relative position

00:20:01.100 --> 00:20:02.400
declining.

00:20:02.400 --> 00:20:08.260
But measured using GDP per capita at current PPP, Europe is not merely keeping pace.

00:20:08.260 --> 00:20:14.300
By that methodology it is actually closing part of the gap with the United States.

00:20:14.300 --> 00:20:18.880
The argument becomes even stronger when we look at hourly productivity.

00:20:18.880 --> 00:20:26.000
At constant prices, Europe's relative productivity has fallen dramatically over the past 25 years,

00:20:26.000 --> 00:20:28.700
represented by the light blue line.

00:20:28.700 --> 00:20:35.800
By using current prices, the Eurozone stood at 86% of US productivity in 2000, compared

00:20:35.800 --> 00:20:44.320
with 87% in 2024 and as recently as 2019 it reached 90%.

00:20:44.320 --> 00:20:47.520
Where does this enormous discrepancy come from?

00:20:47.520 --> 00:20:50.280
Again, we return to technology.

00:20:50.280 --> 00:20:53.600
Let Krukman explain it himself.

00:20:53.600 --> 00:20:58.840
Productivity growth as conventionally measured has infact been much faster in the US than in

00:20:58.840 --> 00:20:59.840
Europe.

00:20:59.840 --> 00:21:04.360
How can this be consistent with the fact that there has been virtually no change in the relative

00:21:04.360 --> 00:21:09.880
value of goods produced per hour, that's the apparent US-Europe paradox. What explains

00:21:09.880 --> 00:21:16.280
it is the fact that the US and European economies produce different mixes of goods.

00:21:16.280 --> 00:21:21.400
One key fact about economic growth in all advanced economies in the 21st century is that

00:21:21.400 --> 00:21:27.600
progress has been highly concentrated in a relatively small sector, the tech or information

00:21:27.600 --> 00:21:35.600
technology, IT sector." According to the data cited by Kroogman, IT represents around

00:21:35.600 --> 00:21:41.520
8% of American value added, but accounts for almost half of US productivity growth.

00:21:42.480 --> 00:21:49.920
What does that mean in practice? Since 1988, our lead productivity in computer manufacturing

00:21:49.920 --> 00:21:54.960
has increased 14 fold. In hospitals by contrast, it has barely changed.

00:21:54.960 --> 00:22:02.760
Does that mean a worker in a computer factory now earns 14 times more relative to a doctor?

00:22:02.760 --> 00:22:05.960
No, so what happened?

00:22:05.960 --> 00:22:07.920
Computers became cheaper.

00:22:07.920 --> 00:22:14.080
The point is that America has a sector in which the quantity of output per worker explodes

00:22:14.080 --> 00:22:17.920
while the price of each unit of output collapses.

00:22:17.920 --> 00:22:24.080
That is why fixed price GDP records such extraordinary American growth.

00:22:24.080 --> 00:22:29.580
But the benefits flow around the world, because American capitalism naturally wants to sell

00:22:29.580 --> 00:22:31.060
its output.

00:22:31.060 --> 00:22:36.540
The result appears in Europe as cheaper computers, cheaper software and cheaper technology, which

00:22:36.540 --> 00:22:41.940
Americans can adapt and use with productivity levels not dramatically different from those

00:22:41.940 --> 00:22:43.880
of Americans.

00:22:43.880 --> 00:22:48.280
This paradox is precisely what we see in the difference between productivity measured at

00:22:48.280 --> 00:22:54.040
constant prices and productivity measured through current purchasing power.

00:22:54.040 --> 00:22:58.940
Krugmann knows that a similar divergence can be even observed within the United States

00:22:58.940 --> 00:22:59.940
itself.

00:22:59.940 --> 00:23:05.160
The difference in productivity growth between California and the rest of America is actually

00:23:05.160 --> 00:23:09.740
greater than the difference between the United States and Europe.

00:23:09.740 --> 00:23:15.920
And yet Texas does not consider itself a quote failed state, while Florida does not publish

00:23:15.920 --> 00:23:18.220
a drug report.

00:23:18.220 --> 00:23:22.280
why, because they are all part of one country.

00:23:22.280 --> 00:23:27.120
None of this means that owning the technology brings no advantages, and Krugman says so

00:23:27.120 --> 00:23:28.700
explicitly.

00:23:28.700 --> 00:23:34.220
Owning technology gives you control over its distribution, and control naturally translates

00:23:34.220 --> 00:23:36.360
into power.

00:23:36.360 --> 00:23:42.460
But the American Nobel laureate argues that most of the economic benefits comes from using AI

00:23:42.460 --> 00:23:45.700
not simply from owning open AI.

00:23:45.700 --> 00:23:51.460
nephew Iglesias, another widely read commentator, added his own perspective to the debate using

00:23:51.460 --> 00:23:56.380
the example of Poland, which can probably be treated here as a broader representative

00:23:56.380 --> 00:24:00.180
of post-communist central and eastern Europe.

00:24:00.180 --> 00:24:05.980
He knows that the Polish or CEE development model has always fascinated economists because

00:24:05.980 --> 00:24:13.140
it looks almost like the textbook version of how economic convergence is supposed to work,

00:24:13.140 --> 00:24:17.900
And yet a model like this has rarely existed in practice.

00:24:17.900 --> 00:24:23.300
Iglesias goes further, conventional wisdom once suggested that a country simply could

00:24:23.300 --> 00:24:29.580
not become truly wealthy using such a model, and what model does he mean?

00:24:29.580 --> 00:24:34.980
One based on innovations developed elsewhere but implemented effectively at home, a model

00:24:34.980 --> 00:24:40.700
based on foreign direct investment, good education and functional institutions.

00:24:40.700 --> 00:24:46.900
In a somewhat ironic sense, Europe as a whole is beginning to resemble Poland or CEE on

00:24:46.900 --> 00:24:48.660
a much larger scale.

00:24:48.660 --> 00:24:54.220
It does not control the main nodes of innovation, but it uses them effectively.

00:24:54.220 --> 00:24:59.060
To sum up this part of the argument, America enjoys an advantage in the nominal value of

00:24:59.060 --> 00:25:06.300
its prosperity and advantage that in recent decades, a large D come from IT and Big Tech.

00:25:06.300 --> 00:25:11.900
But the mechanics of capitalism and globalization mean that America inevitably shares the fruits

00:25:11.900 --> 00:25:17.180
of that revolution while competition forces prices downward.

00:25:17.180 --> 00:25:22.260
Europe benefits from those changes, that is why its hourly productivity remains only modestly

00:25:22.260 --> 00:25:24.420
below the American level.

00:25:24.420 --> 00:25:30.260
At the same time, Europeans intentionally convert some of the hours they could spend producing

00:25:30.260 --> 00:25:36.580
an additional GDP into free time and other activities that are economically worthless from

00:25:36.580 --> 00:25:43.220
the perspective of national accounts such as maternity leave.

00:25:43.220 --> 00:25:47.940
Naturally this reduces GDP per capita PPP across European societies.

00:25:47.940 --> 00:25:53.860
At this point people closely attached to either the American or European social model will

00:25:53.860 --> 00:25:58.380
naturally be tempted to elevate one above the other.

00:25:58.380 --> 00:26:02.900
The problem is that there is no objective way to say that one is simply better.

00:26:02.900 --> 00:26:05.900
The two are in many respects complementary.

00:26:05.900 --> 00:26:09.820
It is more a question of goals than superiority.

00:26:09.820 --> 00:26:15.860
Do Europe and the United States have the same goal, culturally, socially and politically?

00:26:15.860 --> 00:26:17.860
The answer is no.

00:26:17.860 --> 00:26:23.180
Of course, if we consult the US Constitution or the Constitution of European States, we will

00:26:23.180 --> 00:26:30.020
find similar language, liberty, prosperity, development, but legal documents are not a particularly

00:26:30.020 --> 00:26:36.340
useful compass here, because even the meaning of freedom varies from person to person.

00:26:36.340 --> 00:26:41.260
The better answer lies in political and social divisions, and in the cultures that emerge

00:26:41.260 --> 00:26:42.260
from them.

00:26:42.260 --> 00:26:48.980
America leads in capitalism, dynamism, innovation and breaking new barriers.

00:26:48.980 --> 00:26:55.540
More than Europe has increasingly chosen egalitarianism, social balance and stability, and its

00:26:55.540 --> 00:26:57.660
modus vivendi.

00:26:57.660 --> 00:27:04.340
Europe places the average Kowalski, Smith or Miller, closer to the center of the system.

00:27:04.340 --> 00:27:10.180
America places greater emphasis on exceptional individuals, rewards them more heavily and

00:27:10.180 --> 00:27:13.700
holds them up as models to emulate.

00:27:13.700 --> 00:27:16.380
Is either approach inherently better?

00:27:16.380 --> 00:27:19.780
No, that depends on your preferences.

00:27:19.780 --> 00:27:25.140
Do you want to be a decoding catch of innovation and participate directly in the next technological

00:27:25.140 --> 00:27:26.300
revolution?

00:27:26.300 --> 00:27:30.060
You will probably feel more at home in San Francisco.

00:27:30.060 --> 00:27:35.460
Do you want a stable job, perhaps some would lower pay, stronger employment protection and

00:27:35.460 --> 00:27:39.260
more room to focus on life outside work?

00:27:39.260 --> 00:27:41.860
Europe may suit you better.

00:27:41.860 --> 00:27:47.660
will not boast higher GDP per capita than the United States, but it does outperform America

00:27:47.660 --> 00:27:52.840
in a wide range of rankings designed to measure quality of life.

00:27:52.840 --> 00:27:59.020
One example is the Hello Safe Prosperity Ranking, which instead of reliant exclusively on GDP,

00:27:59.020 --> 00:28:06.040
combines five weighted factors, GDP per capita at PPP, G and I per capita, the Human Development

00:28:06.040 --> 00:28:10.520
Index, income inequality, and relative poverty.

00:28:10.520 --> 00:28:15.340
14 European countries are in the top 20, including the top 5.

00:28:15.340 --> 00:28:21.900
The United States, despite its clear advantage in GDP per capita, ranks only 17th.

00:28:21.900 --> 00:28:27.940
In the World Happiness Report, 2026, Europe practically dominates the field.

00:28:27.940 --> 00:28:30.940
15 European countries appear in the top 20.

00:28:30.940 --> 00:28:35.580
The United States ranks 23rd, while China is 65.

00:28:35.580 --> 00:28:43.980
Then there is the 2026 Global Social Progress Index, which deliberately excludes GDP.

00:28:43.980 --> 00:28:49.820
In an index measuring social outcomes alone, including health, safety, education, housing,

00:28:49.820 --> 00:28:57.020
environmental quality, rights and opportunity, Europe occupies 16 of the top 20 positions.

00:28:57.020 --> 00:29:02.700
The United States ranks 30 second, while China ranks 70 third.

00:29:02.700 --> 00:29:08.620
A useful summary of these comparisons is a graphic that circulated wildly some time ago,

00:29:08.620 --> 00:29:12.700
comparing the United Kingdom with the US states.

00:29:12.700 --> 00:29:18.060
If Britain were another American state, it would have the highest life expectancy, the

00:29:18.060 --> 00:29:23.380
lowest homicide rate, the longest maternity leave, the longest education period, and would

00:29:23.380 --> 00:29:26.420
lead on a range of other measures.

00:29:26.420 --> 00:29:29.900
at the same time measured by GDP per capita.

00:29:29.900 --> 00:29:33.400
It would be the poorest state in the US.

00:29:33.400 --> 00:29:39.420
So are these merely, feel-good rankings designed to make Europeans feel better while hiding

00:29:39.420 --> 00:29:41.500
their suppose decline?

00:29:41.500 --> 00:29:44.780
Or do they represent a form of self-power?

00:29:44.780 --> 00:29:51.360
Something harder to measure than GDP or innovation, but not necessarily less important from the perspective

00:29:51.360 --> 00:29:53.960
of an ordinary citizen.

00:29:53.960 --> 00:30:00.280
One can even consider that a better source of long-term cohesion, civilizational stability

00:30:00.280 --> 00:30:02.200
and internal resilience.

00:30:02.200 --> 00:30:07.420
After all, people remain the ultimate foundation of every political and economic system.

00:30:07.420 --> 00:30:13.000
Let's now return to the comparison between the American and European models.

00:30:13.000 --> 00:30:16.680
The differences between them have broader political consequences.

00:30:16.680 --> 00:30:21.920
The choices made by Europe and the United States over recent decades may be more deterministic

00:30:21.920 --> 00:30:24.680
then they initially appear.

00:30:24.680 --> 00:30:29.840
America is the wealthiest and most powerful unified state in the world.

00:30:29.840 --> 00:30:35.040
For a series of mechanisms including the Breton Woods system and the power of the dollar,

00:30:35.040 --> 00:30:40.800
the US Navy and New York financial markets, it has attracted and continues to attract

00:30:40.800 --> 00:30:45.400
capital, talent and innovation from across the planet.

00:30:45.400 --> 00:30:51.360
Its capitalist system then directs those resources toward the most productive and revolutionary

00:30:51.360 --> 00:30:57.400
sectors. On that foundation, the world's most innovative economic sector emerged over

00:30:57.400 --> 00:31:04.480
recent decades, American software and hardware. Yet American power fundamentally grew from

00:31:04.480 --> 00:31:10.640
geography. It emerged from domestic power consolidated through civil wars and conflicts

00:31:10.640 --> 00:31:17.280
with colonial empires. Once the right software, namely capitalism and democracy, was added

00:31:17.280 --> 00:31:24.880
to that geography, global power followed naturally. More than two centuries of American grandeur

00:31:24.880 --> 00:31:30.160
have shaped not only the country's global reach, which is undeniable, but also the mindset

00:31:30.160 --> 00:31:35.360
of generations of American officials, decision makers and ultimately citizens.

00:31:36.560 --> 00:31:43.520
The pursuit of more, better, greater is the default imperative. America was built for greatness,

00:31:43.520 --> 00:31:49.680
and in any lesser role it feels constrained. That incidentally is part of the reason for its

00:31:49.680 --> 00:31:56.240
anxiety over China's rise. China is the only actor with a realistic chance of challenging

00:31:56.240 --> 00:32:03.280
the global dominance of the American models of the Rande. And yet that greatness does not automatically

00:32:03.280 --> 00:32:09.840
mean that the underlying social model is superior. The same system that is exceptionally effective at

00:32:09.840 --> 00:32:15.840
and producing capital, innovation and individuals capable of pushing the frontier also generates

00:32:15.840 --> 00:32:18.400
specific costs.

00:32:18.400 --> 00:32:24.480
Greater social inequality, weaker protection for some citizens and stronger commercialization

00:32:24.480 --> 00:32:29.960
of essential services, mean that a significant part of society increasingly feels that the

00:32:29.960 --> 00:32:33.800
existing social contract no longer serves them.

00:32:33.800 --> 00:32:40.120
Over time, this frustration stops being purely economic and becomes political.

00:32:40.120 --> 00:32:45.120
Trump is probably better understood here as a symptom than as a cause, the product of

00:32:45.120 --> 00:32:52.140
a society in which extraordinary economic dynamism co-existes with a growing sense of inequality

00:32:52.140 --> 00:32:54.320
and unfurness.

00:32:54.320 --> 00:32:55.680
And Europe?

00:32:55.680 --> 00:33:00.520
Why is modern Europe the way it is?

00:33:00.520 --> 00:33:06.680
current mindset, which is obviously very different from the one that existed before the 90th century,

00:33:06.680 --> 00:33:13.160
the age of exploration and the era of quote controlling the world, emerged from the two bloodiest

00:33:13.160 --> 00:33:20.840
wars in human history. It also emerged from Europe's subordination or at best accommodation to two

00:33:20.840 --> 00:33:26.840
dominant global powers. The Brancel's project attempts to reduce the geopolitical weakness of

00:33:26.840 --> 00:33:33.080
individual European states relative to the United States, China and previously the Soviet Union.

00:33:33.080 --> 00:33:39.640
To some extent it succeeds, but because those states remain unwilling to surrender their autonomy

00:33:39.640 --> 00:33:45.560
to a single European state, each continues to feel geopolitically weaker than the major powers.

00:33:46.600 --> 00:33:51.960
I emphasize the word geopolitically, because once again, the fact that one country is

00:33:51.960 --> 00:33:57.840
smaller in nominal or absolute terms does not automatically make it worse or weak.

00:33:57.840 --> 00:34:04.240
Crashed by two catastrophic wars and naturally a war of its absolute weakness compared with

00:34:04.240 --> 00:34:10.360
the superpowers, Europe developed its latest cultural archetype inward.

00:34:10.360 --> 00:34:16.080
It began searching for advantages in more subtle ways and in alignment with the victorious power,

00:34:16.080 --> 00:34:17.640
the United States.

00:34:17.640 --> 00:34:23.680
stopped competing for the direct control over the world, yet that did not mean abandoning

00:34:23.680 --> 00:34:26.380
capitalism, quite the opposite.

00:34:26.380 --> 00:34:30.960
For many years Europe practiced it at least as effectively as its big brother across the

00:34:30.960 --> 00:34:37.800
Atlantic, exporting its products around the world on a massive scale, by the way still does.

00:34:37.800 --> 00:34:42.180
And this would later provoke the anger of Donald Trump, who argued that Europe was taking

00:34:42.180 --> 00:34:46.400
advantage of America, but is that really the case?

00:34:46.400 --> 00:34:51.960
In practice Europe bears a number of significant costs, or put more symbiotically contributes

00:34:51.960 --> 00:34:56.880
materially to America's dominant position, to name just a few.

00:34:56.880 --> 00:35:01.960
Even in peacetime, the current arrangement, despite broadly comparable productivity, effectively

00:35:01.960 --> 00:35:05.960
gives the United States a 10-15% dividend.

00:35:05.960 --> 00:35:10.760
In nominal terms, that amounts to several trillion dollars a year.

00:35:10.760 --> 00:35:16.600
Second, Europe belongs to the same broad civilizational camp and therefore not truly extends

00:35:16.600 --> 00:35:19.440
American power around the world.

00:35:19.440 --> 00:35:22.760
Something the United States is perfectly willing to use.

00:35:22.760 --> 00:35:27.240
And as noted earlier, this leadership is not imposed on America.

00:35:27.240 --> 00:35:32.560
It is a role America actively chooses and treats as its default position.

00:35:32.560 --> 00:35:37.780
Europe's best talent raised and educated in Europe very often ends up powering the most

00:35:37.780 --> 00:35:43.280
innovative sectors of the American economy, the financial value of this transfer is almost

00:35:43.280 --> 00:35:45.400
impossible to calculate.

00:35:45.400 --> 00:35:50.700
In Poland there is this widely shared picture of the open AI team whose core consists of

00:35:50.700 --> 00:35:52.440
several polls.

00:35:52.440 --> 00:35:57.880
Finally, Europe also produces things America for various reasons either does not want to produce

00:35:57.880 --> 00:36:02.120
or cannot easily produce but still needs.

00:36:02.120 --> 00:36:07.680
According to Europe itself, the modern European social contract decided that a certain,

00:36:07.680 --> 00:36:12.360
substantially larger, share of the fruits of the economic activity than in the United States

00:36:12.360 --> 00:36:18.520
should be placed into a common pool, broadly devoted to society and its well-being.

00:36:18.520 --> 00:36:21.560
Naturally, this comes with problems.

00:36:21.560 --> 00:36:26.600
It can produce a somewhat more comfortable, perhaps even less ambitious, society, and create

00:36:26.600 --> 00:36:29.880
incentives to gain the welfare system.

00:36:29.880 --> 00:36:35.120
But on the other side of that bergen is greater social cohesion and a society that is structurally

00:36:35.120 --> 00:36:40.500
less prone to the kind of political and social upheaval that will still come in place just

00:36:40.500 --> 00:36:44.040
a century or a century and a half ago.

00:36:44.040 --> 00:36:49.940
During periods of calm, these two social models therefore existed next one to another, and

00:36:49.940 --> 00:36:52.640
in a sense complemented each other.

00:36:52.640 --> 00:36:59.360
Americans created and exported a product called novelty, profiting from technological revolutions.

00:36:59.360 --> 00:37:05.640
Because both that novelty adapted it and exported more conservative products, for example industrial

00:37:05.640 --> 00:37:11.960
machinery, making money that way, even if somewhat less than the United States.

00:37:11.960 --> 00:37:14.480
Both sides needed each other.

00:37:14.480 --> 00:37:19.720
Europeans knew that salaries were higher in America, houses were larger and technological progress

00:37:19.720 --> 00:37:21.840
often began there.

00:37:21.840 --> 00:37:27.400
Americans in turn knew that life in Europe moved more slowly, incomes were somewhat lower,

00:37:27.400 --> 00:37:33.400
but they also respected European industry and often admired the European way of life.

00:37:33.400 --> 00:37:38.400
Here it is also worth noting that observers and analysts are often tempted to judge Europe

00:37:38.400 --> 00:37:43.640
for a framework similar to the one they used for the United States or China, as if Europe

00:37:43.640 --> 00:37:45.440
were a single state.

00:37:45.440 --> 00:37:51.480
Then when they failed to see that decisiveness and agency, normally associated with an efficient,

00:37:51.480 --> 00:37:57.380
unified political organism, they classified that absence as a European failure.

00:37:57.380 --> 00:38:02.860
But should we punish a cat because it meows instead of barking?

00:38:02.860 --> 00:38:05.900
Europe operates under fundamentally different conditions.

00:38:05.900 --> 00:38:10.420
We often complain that Europeans cannot agree with one another, but is that expectation

00:38:10.420 --> 00:38:11.420
even reasonable?

00:38:11.420 --> 00:38:16.980
At a continental level, Europe struggles to reach consensus because historically, it simply

00:38:16.980 --> 00:38:19.340
never had to.

00:38:19.340 --> 00:38:25.740
The old continent has roughly 4 to 5,000 years of organized political history, dating back

00:38:25.740 --> 00:38:29.300
to the earliest Bronze Age civilizations.

00:38:29.300 --> 00:38:35.820
The experiment of durable, voluntary, super-national integration by comparison is only a few decades

00:38:35.820 --> 00:38:36.820
old.

00:38:36.820 --> 00:38:43.700
76 years have passed since the Schumann Declaration, at most less than 2% of that historical

00:38:43.700 --> 00:38:44.700
period.

00:38:44.700 --> 00:38:52.940
The European Union, in its present form, has existed for 33 years, roughly 0.8%, and only 22

00:38:52.940 --> 00:38:57.940
years have passed since the greatest Eastern enlargement around half a percent.

00:38:57.940 --> 00:39:05.540
So, from an evolutionary perspective, this political organism has practically just been born,

00:39:05.540 --> 00:39:10.300
and that helps us understand the structural problem of Europe, and understanding it better

00:39:10.300 --> 00:39:14.940
allows us to calibrate our political expectations more realistically.

00:39:14.940 --> 00:39:21.180
However, none of this changes the fact that now we live in an era of competing great powers,

00:39:21.180 --> 00:39:27.100
in a fight, unsurprisingly, it is better to be large, strong and preferably caring a club.

00:39:27.100 --> 00:39:33.180
Europeans as a collection of geopolitical normies, could theoretically stand up to a much larger

00:39:33.180 --> 00:39:36.100
opponent if they coordinated effectively.

00:39:36.100 --> 00:39:41.340
A problem is that they started seriously talking to each other about 5 minutes ago, and before

00:39:41.340 --> 00:39:46.340
that they spent most of their history beating each other up.

00:39:46.340 --> 00:39:50.740
So yes, explaining the problem does not make it disappear, but how serious are these

00:39:50.740 --> 00:39:56.300
problems in practice and how much of a threat do they actually pose to Europe.

00:39:56.300 --> 00:40:00.900
The popular view is that Europe is being hit from every direction, does nothing but

00:40:00.900 --> 00:40:06.940
absorb the blows and has turned passivity into an art form.

00:40:06.940 --> 00:40:12.300
So once again, let's step back and look from a wider perspective and start with a geographically

00:40:12.300 --> 00:40:15.420
closest problem, the war in Ukraine.

00:40:15.420 --> 00:40:20.420
At first glance, yes, it is difficult to imagine more dramatic evidence of European weakness than

00:40:20.420 --> 00:40:24.100
the largest war on the continent in 80 years.

00:40:24.100 --> 00:40:30.100
2014 is one of the dates most commonly used to mark the emergence of a new global order,

00:40:30.100 --> 00:40:34.620
an order that supposedly heralds the coming decline of Europe.

00:40:34.620 --> 00:40:39.060
And yes, geographically the war broke out in Europe.

00:40:39.060 --> 00:40:44.260
But it broke out on the periphery of the European model described above, and the challenge came

00:40:44.260 --> 00:40:49.820
from the opposing model, the model of Russian civilization, which incidentally combines almost

00:40:49.820 --> 00:40:55.260
none of the advantages of either the American or European systems, while enthusiastically

00:40:55.260 --> 00:40:59.740
embracing and amplifying many of their weaknesses.

00:40:59.740 --> 00:41:04.020
That Russian model targeted a country that wanted to abandon one system.

00:41:04.020 --> 00:41:08.060
The Russian one enjoyed another, the European one.

00:41:08.060 --> 00:41:14.180
And because of its size and power accumulated in an earlier era governed by different rules,

00:41:14.180 --> 00:41:19.460
the Russian state possessed sufficient resources to attempt such a war.

00:41:19.460 --> 00:41:23.940
From time to time we hear excerpts from Vladimir Sooviev's conversations with Russian

00:41:23.940 --> 00:41:30.060
nationalists, who angrily incises that quote, the West or Europe cost this war.

